Hitchhiking as such is not regulated by German law and is legal on ordinary roads; what the law restricts is where you may stand. [https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/stvo_2013/__18.html § 18(9) of the ''Straßenverkehrs-Ordnung'' (StVO)] forbids pedestrians to set foot on an ''Autobahn'' at all, and lets them cross a ''Kraftfahrstraße'' only at junctions or other places provided for it — that covers the carriageway, the hard shoulder and the on-ramp beyond the blue motorway sign. Drivers cannot legally pick you up there either, because § 18(8) StVO bans stopping on the ''Autobahn'', hard shoulder included. So thumb on the on-ramp ''before'' the blue sign, or ask people face to face at a ''Raststätte'', ''Autohof'' or ''Rastplatz'', which is where hitchhiking on the motorway network actually happens. Service stations are leased businesses and the operator has ''Hausrecht'' over the forecourt, so they may send you off it — the parking area is then the place to stand.
%tail
Just like other people, police may or may not be friendly towards hitchhikers. It is said that police controls are more frequent in the southern states of [[Bavaria]] and [[Baden-Württemberg]]. As a foreigner you have to carry your passport at all times; a national ID card is sufficient if you are from another EU country. If you are being checked, be friendly, show it to them and they will leave you alone.
